  "query_preview": "..\nTuesday night through Friday: A weak shortwave tracking through\nnorthern British Columbia temporarily flattens the ridge of high\npressure over the Inland Northwest Tuesday night into Wednesday,\nbringing increased cloud cover regionwide and a 15% chance of \nshowers along the Cascade crest. Clouds clear Wednesday \nafternoon as high pressure re-amplifies in the wake of the \nshortwave. \n\nOn Thursday, a thermal trough developing west of the Cascades will\ndrive an easterly pressure gradient across Central and Eastern \nWA. Expect sustained easterly winds around 10 mph and gusts of \n15 to 20 mph across the Columbia Basin.\n\nDry conditions persist through Friday as temperatures trend \nwarmer under the influence of the upper-level ridge. Afternoon \nhigh temperatures peak Friday in the upper 60s to mid 70s, 10 \nto 15 degrees above average high temperatures for mid- October. \nOvernight low temperatures will trend warmer as well. By Friday \nnight, lows will range from the mid 30s for the sheltered \nnorthern valleys to the upper 40s further south.\n\nSaturday through Monday: A more active pattern returns heading\ninto the weekend as a system approaches the coast of British \nColumbia, displacing the upper-level ridge and initiating a \ncooling trend. Highs will range from the low to upper 60s for \nmost locations Saturday and Sunday, then will drop into the 50s \nby Monday. Moisture associated with the system will remain \nlargely north in British Columbia and Alberta, restricting \nprecipitation chances for the Inland Northwest to areas of \nhigher terrain. The Cascade crest will see a 40 to 60 percent \nchance of showers Saturday, with a 15 percent chance of showers \nexpanding eastward across the northern mountains and Idaho \nPanhandle Saturday afternoon.\n\nThe primary impact from Saturday's system will be gusty westerly\nwinds across the Columbia Basin and Waterville Plateau eastward\ninto the Spokane/Coeur d'Alene areas. For the aforementioned\nareas, expect sustained speeds of 15 to 25 mph and gusts up to\n40 mph. Given the lack of widespread wetting rains, localized\nblowing dust will be a concern with these winds. \n\nA deeper low pressure system moves onshore Monday into Tuesday,\nbut ensemble clusters are not yet in agreement on the strength\nand track of this low. Around 70 percent of ensemble members\nfavor a more direct path over the Inland Northwest, which would\nbring widespread precipitation chances and a noticeable drop in\ntemperatures. The other 30 percent of solutions suggest the low\nwill drop south along the coastline before moving inland over\nCalifornia. This would result in a drier forecast with more\nsubtle cooling. /Fewkes",
